module tasks.`2026-11-27-browser-over-mcp`

task BrowserOverMcp {
  title "Built-in browser over MCP: bundled Chromium, a browser host process, and browser tools a seat can call"
  verify $ make test-browser
  paths "crates/**", "Makefile", "scripts/**", "verification/**", "packaging/**", "Cargo.toml", "Cargo.lock", "ATTRIBUTION.md", "AGENTS.md", "LAND.md", ".bboxy/specs/licensing.md"
  brief """
    # Built-in browser over MCP

    ROLE: EXECUTOR. Operator, 2026-11-27: "implement all of this, dig deep, close all the gaps, go hard". Delegated by the sudo seat (Vesper Greywind).

    Create a goal for this task now (Codex create_goal): "make test-browser exits 0 and the change is landed on origin/main". Hold it until both are true.

    Our stand: subtract before you add; minimize concepts; report what you removed.

    ## Outcome

    A seat calls MCP tools and drives the Chromium that ships inside bboxy450-term: open a page, run Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) commands, get a screenshot back as an artifact. It works in the installed release app and in an unattended test, with no window, no network and no operator.

    ## What exists (read these first)

    - crates/bboxy450-cef: defaults.rs (our settings and switches: windowless, private cache root, mock keychain, no remote-debugging port), cdp.rs (pure CDP client: numbers requests, matches each reply to one request, routes events), engine.rs (settings(), app(), send(), observer(): CDP bytes through CEF's in-process DevTools channel). Feature engine pulls cef =156.7.0.
    - crates/bboxy450-term: feature browser = bboxy450-cef/engine; today it only prints the CEF version (src/entry.rs:19, src/main.rs:10285).
    - Makefile:451-459: release builds pass --features browser unless BROWSER=0; debug builds never do. scripts/cache-env.sh:42 sets CEF_PATH (the shared CEF distribution, ~/Library/Caches/bboxy450-cef/156.0.31/cef_macos_aarch64, which holds Chromium Embedded Framework.framework).
    - The cef crate ships its own bundling help: src/build_util, src/bin/bundle-cef-app, library_loader.rs, sandbox.rs, and an accelerated_osr feature (in ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/cef-156.7.0+156.0.31).
    - MCP tools live in crates/bbt-mux/src/mcp/ (definitions per file, for example seats.rs:82 and sudo.rs:45; dispatch in logic). python_execute and the artifact tools (mcp/artifacts.rs) show how a tool runs a child process and stores bytes as an artifact.

    ## What is missing (observed 2026-11-27 on the installed app from 1d349fedf)

    - ~/Applications/bboxy450-term.app has no Contents/Frameworks: the Chromium framework and CEF helper apps are not bundled, so Chromium cannot start.
    - No process starts CEF. No MCP tool reaches it. tools/list has nothing named browser.

    ## Build this, in this order

    1. Bundle. make bundle-files (and so make install, make install-unsigned when built with the browser feature, make release) puts Chromium Embedded Framework.framework and the CEF helper apps where CEF expects them inside bboxy450-term.app, signed inside-out before the outer bundle is signed. The hardened-runtime entitlements the helpers need (JIT and friends) go in packaging/. A build with BROWSER=0 stays exactly as it is today.
    2. Browser host. One subcommand, bboxy450-term browser-host, compiled only with feature browser: it starts CEF windowless with bboxy450_cef::defaults (private cache root given by its parent, no port), opens one browser, and carries CDP over stdio: one JSON CDP message per line in, replies and events one per line out. It exits when stdin closes. The same executable serves as CEF's subprocess entry when CEF re-executes it, or uses the helper apps: choose what CEF on macOS actually requires and say which.
    3. MCP tools, available to every seat class, each bounded like the other tools (results under the existing result budget, with a cut note):
       - browser_open {url}: starts this seat's browser if needed (one browser per seat, started lazily, stopped when the seat closes or the app stops backend owners), navigates, waits for load, returns the final url, title and http status.
       - browser_cdp {method, params?, timeout_ms?}: sends one CDP command to this seat's browser and returns its result or error, plus the events received since the last call (bounded, oldest dropped and counted).
       - browser_screenshot {full_page?}: Page.captureScreenshot stored as a PNG artifact; returns the artifact id so a reply can embed it.
       - browser_close {}: stops this seat's browser and removes its private profile.
       If fewer tools do the same job with less to learn, ship fewer and say why.
       Without the browser feature the tools are still listed and answer one clear error naming the build flag; never a hang.
    4. Unattended proof. A new Make target, test-browser: it builds what it needs with the browser feature, installs a private ad-hoc bundle (as make smoke does, never ~/Applications), and through the real MCP door of that private app: opens a data: URL page, reads document.title with browser_cdp Runtime.evaluate, takes a screenshot and checks the stored PNG decodes and shows the page's known background colour, closes the browser, and checks no browser-host or helper process it started is left. Also: a second seat's browser cannot see the first seat's page; killing the host mid-request returns an error and the next browser_open starts a fresh host. No network, no Keychain prompt, no visible window. make land runs test-browser when the diff touches crates/bboxy450-cef, the browser-host code, the browser tools or the bundle recipe; it stays out of the default make test unless it costs under two minutes warm.
    5. Formal. The host lifecycle is stateful (absent, starting, ready, request in flight, crashed, closing; restart; a reply after cancel; seat close during a request; A to B to A across a restart). Add its model with a broken control that drops the reply-to-request match or the kill-on-close guard, map it in verification/contracts.toml and verification/coverage.toml, and name the Rust or process test that witnesses each property. make test-formal stays green.
    6. Words. Put the wire contract (stdio lines, tool arguments and results, lifecycle, limits, failure answers) in crates/bboxy450-cef/README.md. Add one sentence about the browser tools to the seat skill crates/bbt-mux/src/adapters/skills/bboxy450/SKILL.md. Keep ATTRIBUTION.md and .bboxy/specs/licensing.md right for anything newly bundled (CEF and Chromium licences ship in the app).

    ## Rules

    - Work only in your prepared worktree. Signed commits. Follow AGENTS.md and LAND.md: land with make land when green; Cargo.lock changes need the full make test once on the final commit.
    - CPU and disk are shared: at most one build or test at a time, niced where the Makefile does it; the disk has about 20 GB free, so reuse CEF_PATH and kache, never download Chromium twice, and delete your own stale build output as you go.
    - Never start the operator's installed app, never touch ~/Applications, never inject keys or capture the operator's screen. Kill only PIDs you started.
    - Not in this task: drawing the page in a pane (that is tasks/2026-11-27-browser-pane.den, which builds on this one). Do not add a remote-debugging port.
    - Latitude: names, file layout and the exact tool set are yours, inside the outcome above. Stop and message only if CEF on macOS cannot run windowless from this bundle at all; send the evidence and the smallest decision needed.

    ## Report

    Write reports/2026-11-27-browser-over-mcp.den, then task_report with the summary, the commit on origin/main, and every command with its real exit status. Include make tool-usage DAYS=1 output.
    """

